WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:12.720
The second design pattern for input controls is the toggle switch. We probably all know it and have used it, and we

2
00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:22.940
know how it behaves: it switches on and off like this. Let's go through some guidelines. A toggle switch is a digital

3
00:00:22.940 --> 00:00:33.160
on/off switch that changes something in the system directly, like a light switch when I enter a room: I press it and

4
00:00:33.160 --> 00:00:43.380
the light goes off or on instantly, so I start something in the system right now. Unlike checkboxes and radio buttons, which

5
00:00:43.380 --> 00:00:53.600
we said don't do anything instantly. So it can sometimes replace radio buttons and checkboxes, but we know the difference: it acts instantly.

6
00:00:53.600 --> 00:01:04.060
Based on that, let's see the guidelines related to it. In this example I'm booking a flight and choosing the airport to

7
00:01:04.060 --> 00:01:14.520
leave from, and the option under it is to search only the airports near me, and that option is enabled with a toggle

8
00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:24.980
switch: I turn it on or off. Is using a toggle here right or wrong? It isn't the best choice, because pressing it

9
00:01:24.980 --> 00:01:35.440
doesn't do anything instantly. I'm changing a setting, like a checkbox or radio button, and only when I finish the search and reach

10
00:01:35.440 --> 00:01:45.900
the results page do I see the toggle's effect. That's not what toggle guidelines say; a toggle's effect is instant, like airplane mode.

11
00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:57.067
Also from this example we get another guideline for the toggle switch: write the label next to the toggle as short and clear

12
00:01:57.067 --> 00:02:08.233
as possible. Users only read what they feel they need to read. If I make the label long, and it contains a negative

13
00:02:08.233 --> 00:02:19.400
too, the user needs time to read and understand it. But if I make the label short and direct, they understand it faster.

14
00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:30.500
From this example we also get another insight: if I press this toggle, am I switching it off, or switching it to on while it's currently off? We might get confused about

15
00:02:30.500 --> 00:02:41.600
which of the two states it's in. That leads to the guideline: if I have two states, on and off, and the label or title is written above rather than beside the

16
00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:52.700
toggle, I can write both states, on the right and off on the left for example, so it's clear it's now off and will go to on. Or the label is written

17
00:02:52.700 --> 00:03:03.800
beside it, so I know that when it's on the left in gray or a neutral color it's off, and when it moves right and takes the primary color it's on. But

18
00:03:03.800 --> 00:03:14.900
if I have two states, it's always better to write both off and on, not just one of them, so no one mistakes that single off or on for the toggle's label.
